WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.550
Our next guest is an enormously talented,

2
00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:07.660
Golden Globe nominated actor who stars in 'West Side Story',

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.840
directed by none other than Steven Spielberg.

4
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:11.673
Take a look.

5
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.000
I want to be unlike how I was.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.850
'Cause I was disappearing down a sewer

7
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.580
and taking you and everything with me.

8
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.980
Yeah, he heard you.

9
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.283
I had time. Lots of it, locked up like that.

10
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.690
And for the first time ever,

11
00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.513
I took a look inside and that was rough doing that,

12
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.520
but I,

13
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.633
I got to stay with myself.

14
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:42.174
(audience cheering and clapping)

15
00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:44.307
It is Ansel Elgort.

16
00:00:44.307 --> 00:00:48.000
And I gladly traveled to chat with him

17
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.120
to his childhood theater camp,

18
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.450
where he starred in a production of 'West Side Story'

19
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.570
almost 15 years ago.

20
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.190
It's very full circle.

21
00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.860
He graces the screen with such talent and such presence,

22
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.860
not since Marlon Brando in 'On The Waterfront',

23
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.350
have I seen such a revelation.

24
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:08.060
He is a triple threat.

25
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.020
Ansel Elgort began at the very place

26
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.000
that I'm about to take you to.

27
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.640
Here's our on-location conversation

28
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.203
with the incredible Ansel Elgort.

29
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.060 line:15%
This is my first time back at Stagedoor Manor

30
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.560 line:15%
in 10 years.

31
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.120
I spent seven summers here

32
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.520
when I was between the ages of 10 and 17.

33
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.270 line:15%
It's really nostalgic.

34
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.870 line:15%
A little smaller than I remembered it, to be honest.

35
00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.510
Wow. And the ladders.

36
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:41.863
Oh, hello.

37
00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:44.173
Nice to meet you.

38
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:46.670
Thanks for coming.

39
00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.553
I know it was a long ride.

40
00:01:47.553 --> 00:01:50.820
I would have traveled to the moon for you.

41
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.230
I can't take the end of the movie.

42
00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.070
Oh my God. Yeah.

43
00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.610
You saw the movie.
Yes.

44
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.143
You're seeing me a little bit as Tony?

45
00:01:57.143 --> 00:01:57.976
(Drew and Ansel chuckling)

46
00:01:57.976 --> 00:01:59.610
A lot. A lot.

47
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.730
Well, I still see you in E.T, so.

48
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.950
You're Tony and I'm Gertie?
Yeah.

49
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.740
Well then that's the perfect
Those are good characters.

50
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:07.940
Bridge.

51
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.180
Did Steven Spielberg change your life?

52
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:11.490
Yeah, definitely.

53
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.340
I just got off the phone with Steven.

54
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.710
We had a long conversation about it

55
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.630
and I can't wait to get into it with you

56
00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.110
because it's so much more than a film.

57
00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.160
Here, let's get chairs.

58
00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.410
You had,

59
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:27.450
what I would think would be that perfect balance

60
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.420
of growing up, learning, getting to be a kid

61
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.340
and then going out there in the world

62
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.440
with a skill set and a developed mind.

63
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.060 line:15%
I'm really, really fortunate and grateful that my, yeah,

64
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:45.400 line:15%
my parents like sent me to ballet school, sent me here.

65
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.280
I was going to like private school when I was a little kid

66
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.910
but then I was, like, the worst student.

67
00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.680
And when I sort of was like, clearly, you know,

68
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.010
singing 'Fiddler On The Roof' at Thanksgiving dinner

69
00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:55.843
and stuff like that,

70
00:02:55.843 --> 00:02:58.470
they were like, okay, maybe you're better at other things.

71
00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.760
And, and I begged,

72
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.010
please take me out of that school.

73
00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:02.180
No problem.

74
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.260
The amount of support that
Really?

75
00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.076
Yeah. The amount of support that they've given me

76
00:03:05.076 --> 00:03:05.970
You're lucky
is just-

77
00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.320
So I'm so, so, so lucky

78
00:03:08.204 --> 00:03:09.357
and coming here was a big part of that.

79
00:03:09.357 --> 00:03:11.708
Well, and you did 'West Side Story'

80
00:03:11.708 --> 00:03:12.623
on this stage.
When you were 12?

81
00:03:12.623 --> 00:03:14.470
On this stage. Yeah.

82
00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:15.510
I was Action.

83
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.090
So I sung Officer Krupke

84
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.630
and you know, kicked around a trashcan

85
00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.460
and we were all like 12 years old.

86
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.530
You know, dancing in the gym,

87
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.330
wearing the Converse, the whole thing.

88
00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.500
How can you put into words

89
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.390
that full circle moment of doing the film?

90
00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.730
What is the difference between twelve-year-old you,

91
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:36.740
Stagedoor Manor doing 'West Side Story'

92
00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:38.910
and 20 something year old you

93
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.550
doing 'West Side Story' with Steven Spielberg?

94
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.920
This place creates a professional atmosphere

95
00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.870
where everyone takes it really seriously,

96
00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.410
but then also everyone loves it.

97
00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:48.930
And the same thing on 'West Side Story',

98
00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.740
it's like, yes, it was a professional at-

99
00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.300
It was a real professional atmosphere, but like,

100
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.410
no one is there because it's a job.

101
00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.360
Everyone is there because like,

102
00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.360
this is our dreams coming true.

103
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.910
Everything changed when I worked with Steven

104
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:04.610
and I've developed this relationship with the camera,

105
00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:09.300
like it's this friend and I don't do things for the camera.

106
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.580
It's just a part of the circle that we all need.

107
00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.740
Being at Stagedoor Manor,

108
00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.490
like, there's a theatricality to what you do.

109
00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.850
And sometimes you actually do things for the audience.

110
00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:22.230
You know, they always say like do it for the back row.

111
00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.080
So that was actually a big transition for me

112
00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.140
because working with Steven,

113
00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.750
sometimes I was doing things like almost for the camera

114
00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:30.230
and he'd be like, stop it.

115
00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:31.499
'Cause I would want to perform.

116
00:04:31.499 --> 00:04:32.332
Uh huh.

117
00:04:32.332 --> 00:04:33.290
But then it's like movie time

118
00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:34.640
or just like you and me now,

119
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.010
it's like, well, forget about the camera.

120
00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:37.940
Just be real. Let it get what it gets.

121
00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:39.510
And that's very, that's very,

122
00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.480
what Stephen was preaching on 'West Side Story'.

123
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.790
I remember we were shooting Maria

124
00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.850
and the same kind of thing.

125
00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.360
I was like, what can I bring to this?

126
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:49.760
And I said, okay, let's, let's try one thing.

127
00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.470
And I ran up the stairs, slid down the banister,

128
00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:56.190
did a turn, Maria, you know what it was?

129
00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:57.550
So, and he was like,

130
00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.789
and the crew, kind of was like,

131
00:04:58.789 --> 00:04:59.949
(claps) oh, that was good.

132
00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:00.782
Right.
We really like that.

133
00:05:00.782 --> 00:05:02.370
And Steven said, okay,

134
00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.050
I'm glad you got that out of your system.

135
00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:05.770
(Drew laughing)

136
00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.250
After lunch, when we come back,

137
00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.620
you're going to walk from there to there like a regular guy.

138
00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.420
And just say the words that like, like, as if,

139
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.770
if we were walking down the street in New York city,

140
00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:15.780
you might see you and say,

141
00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.110
oh, that guy looks like he's having a good day,

142
00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.199
but nothing more than that.
Mm hmm.

143
00:05:19.199 --> 00:05:20.680
And he was, and he was right.

144
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.090
Even with Steven Spielberg,

145
00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.810
you can fail and he'll catch you, which is nice.

146
00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:29.160
He pointed out every time I failed, which was great.

147
00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.370
I remember one time we were in the Rumble,

148
00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:32.520
take three or four.

149
00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.370
I still hadn't done what Steven wanted.

150
00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:38.110
And he just came over to me,

151
00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.197
grabbed my hands and said,

152
00:05:40.197 --> 00:05:41.683
"You're not bringing it.

153
00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.110
You're not bringing it."

154
00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:49.020
Next take, boom, emotion, no problem.

155
00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.010
He knows. He knows.

156
00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.980
He knows how to do it
That's what we need.

157
00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:54.500
I love that we're in jobs

158
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.620
that allow us to become completely different people

159
00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.580
from one second to another.

160
00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.990
I live for directors to come at me

161
00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.310
and helped shape me, mold me.

162
00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.130
George Clooney literally came up to me,

163
00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.040
he goes, if you take your eyes off Sam Rockwell

164
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.780
for one second, I'm cutting.

165
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.940
One second. It was the best take I did in the whole movie.

166
00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.010
What scene did you go home and think,

167
00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.500
I totally didn't get that thing I wanted.

168
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.150
I sort of did that every night.

169
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.890
But you know, you have to kind of accept

170
00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:25.380
that what you do is what you do.

171
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.390
That's the only frustrating thing about movies

172
00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.000
is that you don't get to go back and do it again.

173
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.590
That's why I kind of want to do Broadway

174
00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.050
and Broadway Next or something like that.

175
00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:38.050
So, you get offered any thing on Broadway

176
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:42.200
that you want to go do next. What do you do?

177
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.140
What's your dream play?

178
00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:44.860
I love 'Guys and Dolls'.

179
00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.690
I've already, I did it in high school,

180
00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.420
but, you know, that was like four performances

181
00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:50.690
or eight performances or something.

182
00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.310
I'd like to do a long run of a show like that,

183
00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.150
where you get to sing, you get to act.

184
00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.007
You say in the film,

185
00:06:57.007 --> 00:07:00.820
"I want to be unlike how I was" in the first scene

186
00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.210
when we meet Tony.

187
00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.800
Is there anything you would like to be unlike yourself

188
00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.080
at some point in your life?

189
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:09.930
On 'West Side Story',

190
00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.860
there was a security guard, Muhammad,

191
00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.960
who out of nowhere came up to me and said,

192
00:07:15.287 --> 00:07:19.560
"You're going to need to find a rock to crack your ego on."

193
00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.770
It was like some Zen stuff, you know?

194
00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.290
And I'm like, nice to meet you, you know,

195
00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:24.123
I'm Ansel.

196
00:07:24.123 --> 00:07:24.956
He's like I'm Muhammad.

197
00:07:24.956 --> 00:07:27.420
And then every time I'd see him throughout the movie,

198
00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.680
he'd say, "Have you found that rock yet?"

199
00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.530
So I'm definitely working on that.

200
00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:34.830
Does your girlfriend and your mother and your father,

201
00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.560
are they some type of a rock?

202
00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:38.450
Definitely. Yeah.

203
00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:39.593
People you love.

204
00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.310
And I also just think it's like a matter of perspective.

205
00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:45.060
My mother is a pretty great person

206
00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:48.390
and she is loving to everyone

207
00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:50.010
and open to the world around her

208
00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.170
and doesn't really have an ego.

209
00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.940
And so I'd definitely aspire to be like her.

210
00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.996
My father's ego is through the roof, but he's hilarious.

211
00:07:56.996 --> 00:07:58.296
(Drew laughing)

212
00:07:58.296 --> 00:08:00.110
I heard he has a great sense of humor.

213
00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:00.943
Yeah. Amazing.

214
00:08:00.943 --> 00:08:02.350
Yeah. He's the funniest guy.

215
00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:03.210
Also just,

216
00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:05.740
I feel like these days I've been coming more and more

217
00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:06.760
to realization.

218
00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.470
I've been seeing the world more from a bird's eye view

219
00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.030
where we're like, actually,

220
00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:11.730
we're all kind of, like, meaningless.

221
00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.490
So it's ridiculous for you to put yourself on a pedestal

222
00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:15.350
and think you're special,

223
00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.930
or everyone is just as special as everyone else.

224
00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:19.210
Like 'Oklahoma'.

225
00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:21.250
There's a line from 'Oklahoma'.

226
00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:24.390
♪ I won't say I'm no better than anybody else ♪

227
00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:27.600
♪ But I'll be damned if I ain't half as good ♪

228
00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:28.800
So, you know,
Yes.

229
00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:29.633
You know, we're all,

230
00:08:29.633 --> 00:08:30.740
we're all just as good as each other

231
00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:33.190
and you know, no better and that's it,

232
00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.071
that's the vibe.

233
00:08:34.071 --> 00:08:37.730
(audience cheering and clapping)

234
00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.150
When we come back,

235
00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.230
Ansel gives us the grand tour
(audience member screams)

236
00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.313
of Stagedoor.

237
00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:45.270
Right? Yeah. I know I'm telling you,

238
00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.373
this is what he brings out in people.

239
00:08:48.264 --> 00:08:50.847
(upbeat music)

